   "text": "[reference generation + audio reference] <Picture 1> is the character turnaround sheet of S1, Dohyun, a Korean man in his mid-thirties; it is used only for his identity: face, eyes, short black hair with a natural side part, clean-shaven skin, slim build, slate-blue button-up shirt with the sleeves rolled to the forearms, navy trousers, black leather shoes, and a black wristwatch on his LEFT wrist. <Picture 2> is the exact fixed view of the video: a car dashcam looking out through the windshield of a car parked nose-out in an underground apartment parking garage, with the dark dashboard band across the bottom of the frame, a wide polished stone-tile aisle in front, a row of parked cars against a dark gray wall, gray concrete pillars with yellow-black chevron corner guards, a slatted dark ceiling and long linear LED light strips. <Audio 1> is a recording of S1's own voice saying a different line; it defines S1's voice identity only: the same timbre, pitch, warmth and natural Korean speaking rhythm. S1 speaks with exactly that voice, and the words he says are only his lines written below. The whole video is one continuous recording from the dashcam in <Picture 2>: completely static camera, slightly wide lens, flat dashcam image with the plain cool garage light, no camera movement at all. Throughout the entire video S1 is the identical person from <Picture 1> — the exact same face, eyes, hairstyle and outfit. Never replace S1 with a different person. This identity lock applies ONLY to the face, hair and outfit — it never restricts poses, expressions, movement or energy.\n\nintegrated_multimodal_description: [Shot 1] Fixed dashcam view of <Picture 2>. Two men stand in the aisle just in front of the car. S1 stands close to the camera at the left side of the frame, so near that only his torso is in the picture: his slate-blue shirt, his rolled sleeves and his hands, with his head and face above the top edge of the frame. S2, a second man, stands at the far right edge of the frame, and only his shoulder and arm in a matte charcoal work jacket are visible; his head and face stay outside the frame for the entire video. S2 asks in a low, rough, clipped Korean male voice: <d>[Korean] 살아 있으면?</d>\nAt 00:01.800, S1 moves his left hand once, so the black wristwatch on his left wrist catches the light, and answers flatly in a calm mid-low voice: <d>[Korean] 그럼 보험금 못 받아.</d>\nAt 00:03.800, S2 asks in the same low, rough voice: <d>[Korean] 너 진짜 괜찮겠어?</d>\nAt 00:05.400, S1 stays standing at the left side, only his torso visible, and shifts his weight toward the aisle as if about to step away, until the end.\n\noverall_soundscape: The low hum of the underground garage ventilation, a faint electrical buzz from the LED strips, S1's footsteps on the stone tiles, a slight compressed small-microphone quality as recorded by a dashcam. Only the two men's voices are heard.\n\nnon_diegetic_music: N/A"
